4},"winningTime":{"current":"05:31:33","previous":"05:35:34","deltaSeconds":-241},"avgTime":{"current":"10:23:04","previous":"10:24:09","deltaSeconds":-65}}},"historicalRank":{"fieldSizeRank":1,"finishersRank":5,"finishRateRank":91,"winningTimeRank":28,"winningTimeRankDir":10,"totalRaces":98},"recordContext":{"womenRecords":["up"],"womenPreviousRecord":{"time":"06:09:24","holder":"Elena NURGALIEVA","year":2006,"type":"up"},"womenWouldHaveWonMen":{"mostRecentYear":1974,"mensWinner":"Derek PREISS","mensTime":"06:02:49"}},"narrative":"The 2019 Comrades Marathon was the 94th running of the race, run in the 'up' direction from Durban to Pietermaritzburg over 86.830 kms. The race was held on Sunday, 9 June. Conditions: Mild at start after early morning rain. Cool and overcast with heavy mist in places until midday. Warm winter sunshine all afternoon. Runners had 12 hours to complete the course.\n\nEdward MOTHIBI claimed the men's title in 05:31:33, ahead of Bongmusa MTHEMBU (05:31:58) and Nao KAZAMI (05:39:16). Gerda STEYN won the women's race in 05:58:53, followed by Alexandra MOROZOVA (06:17:40).\n\nGerda STEYN set a new women's up record of 05:58:53, surpassing Elena NURGALIEVA's 06:09:24 from 2006. Gerda STEYN's 05:58:53 was faster than the men's winning time as recently as 1974 (Derek PREISS, 06:02:49).\n\nFrom a field of 24,632 entries, 16,462 runners crossed the finish line (66.8% completion rate), with athletes representing over 19 countries. The field comprised 13,403 men and 3,059 women (18.6% female). The average finish time was 10:23:04.\n\nMedals awarded: 20 Gold, 7 Wally Hayward, 563 Silver, 5,185 Bill Rowan, 4,282 Bronze, 6,405 Vic Clapham. 4,010 runners (24.4%) were completing their first Comrades. The final finisher was Zukiswa MABULU in 12:00:00.\n\nThis edition featured the 1st largest field in race history and the 5th most finishers ever. Compared to the previous up run in 2017, the field was 14.6% larger.\n\nVeterans and masters athletes made up 58% of the finishers, with 6,917 seniors (avg 10:13:05) and 9,545 in the veteran categories.","countryDataPct":100.0}